What exists in this x86-64 machine execution
============================================

Purpose and authority
---------------------
This is a source-level description of how to READ the captured x86-64
disassembly, not a declaration that the English translator is complete or
executable. The authoritative sources are (1) the byte-identical original
object/executable listings under the parent directory; (2) the Intel 64 and
IA-32 Software Developer Manuals, especially Volumes 1 and 2 for instruction
form and semantics, at
https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/intel-sdm.html;
and (3) the System V AMD64 ABI convention for the observed Debian x86-64
harness. This is a *specific historical compiler output*: GCC/GFortran
14.2.0, x86-64 Debian 13, object flags -O2 -g -fno-inline -fPIC.

Define a machine state before an instruction
---------------------------------------------
A. The execution index: address of next machine instruction; it is a BYTE
   address, not the ordinal number of an instruction. The fetched encoding
   can occupy a different number of bytes than its neighbor, with a maximum
   instruction length of 15 bytes in x86-64 long mode. For a successfully
   completed instruction with no branch/call/return/fault, advance it by
   that instruction's decoded byte length. Do not treat an objdump offset
   as an executed event.
B. General-purpose registers: RAX, RBX, RCX, RDX, RSI, RDI, RSP, RBP,
   R8 through R15 and the architectural instruction-pointer register RIP.
   Their useful common view here is 64 bits. EAX and corresponding E*
   and R8D names address low 32 bits; writing a 32-bit general-purpose
   register in 64-bit mode clears the upper 32 bits. AX, AH/AL and other
   smaller aliases have their separately defined partial-write rules.
   Sign extension is different from zero extension.
C. Condition flags (RFLAGS): carry (CF), parity (PF), auxiliary carry
   (AF), zero (ZF), sign (SF), overflow (OF), with exact defined,
   preserved or undefined cases specific to each instruction. Numeric
   comparisons must say whether they are signed (SF/OF) or unsigned
   (CF/ZF). Floating-point compare flags use their own mapping.
D. Vector and scalar-floating registers: XMM0 through XMM15 (128-bit
   architectural views for these recorded SSE instructions), with
   double-precision values in the low 64-bit lane for scalar forms.
   Never call the whole 128-bit register "a floating-point number".
   Packed instructions can operate on all its bits or both 64-bit lanes.
E. Floating-point environment: MXCSR rounding control, sticky exception
   status, exception masks and other applicable state. Results from
   IEEE-754 operations must distinguish rounding and NaN/infinity/
   signed-zero/exception behavior from ideal real arithmetic. The
   recording does not declare the running environment's MXCSR bits.
F. Memory: a byte-addressed space with read/write/executable permissions.
   A QWORD PTR is an eight-byte memory access, DWORD PTR is four bytes,
   XMMWORD PTR is sixteen, and a source operand may be a register,
   immediate number or memory location. Effective-address expressions
   may contain a base register, an index register, a scale and displacement.
   A RIP-relative displacement is relative to the address of the NEXT
   instruction. Machine-endian storage on this x86-64 host is little endian.
G. Control stack: RSP holds a machine address. Ordinary 64-bit push
   allocates eight bytes by decrementing RSP, then stores an eight-byte
   value. Pop reads eight bytes and advances RSP; special overlap cases
   are not admitted by these prose abbreviations. Call and return have
   separate return-address effects and are not mere branch synonyms.
   Alignment and calling conventions matter at external boundaries.
H. External environment: the operating system, dynamic linker, PLT/GOT,
   memory mapping, signal/exception machinery, BLAS, libc, libgfortran
   and libm. The disassembly does not contain a complete definition of
   all these processes.

Instruction spelling is evidence, not a language definition
----------------------------------------------------------
Each line preserves the Intel-syntax disassembly and the ORIGINAL bytes.
"xmm0", "DWORD PTR", "rip", "je" or "dqrdc2_" are source data; the
English operation has a long descriptive name that does not require
readers to know the abbreviated spelling. Explain the words *and*
preserve exact bytes for checking.

For any two-operand Intel-syntax integer instruction, the left operand
is conventionally the destination. A special-case instruction can use
implicit operands, and floating instructions do not all treat their
upper vector lanes the same way. The width of a written destination
must be determined from both operands and the actual decoded encoding,
not guessed from the human-readable mnemonic alone.

Named primitive operations used by the first-pass ledger
---------------------------------------------------------
Move bytes/value:
- "Copy the source value to the destination, according to its
  architectural width" is MOV. It normally does not modify flags.
  The 32-bit destination-zero-extension and memory faults still apply.
- "Read a signed four-byte integer and extend its sign into an
  eight-byte register" is MOVSXD.
- "Calculate the effective address without dereferencing it" is LEA.
  LEA is arithmetic on address expression operands, not a memory load;
  it does not change flags.
- "Copy a low 64-bit scalar floating payload" is MOVSD. For legacy
  SSE, a register destination with a register source preserves the
  upper 64 bits of the destination, whereas a memory-to-register form
  zeroes them. The memory-source/destination cases and operand widths
  must be resolved from encoding.
- "Copy 128 aligned packed floating bits" is MOVAPD/MOVAPS: payload
  copying, not a mathematical calculation; 16-byte alignment of a
  memory operand is relevant.
- "Copy a 64-bit payload between general/vector registers or memory"
  is MOVQ. Register and memory forms have different upper-lane rules.

Control stack and entry:
- "Allocate one eight-byte stack slot and write a value there" is PUSH.
  If source uses RSP as an address component, resolve source using
  the pre-decrement state.
- "Read the stack's top eight-byte slot and restore the stack pointer"
  is POP; register write ordering matters for special forms.
- "Save the return execution address then enter the called routine"
  is CALL. A call through a linker relocation does not target the
  printed unlinked placeholder address. A call through PLT can cross
  into a runtime library that is not in the listing.
- "Restore the saved return execution address and transfer execution
  to it" is RET; malformed stack/target can fault.
- "Continue at a branch target instead of the sequential next
  instruction" is JMP.
- "Do no architecturally useful computation, advance by the full
  encoded length" is NOP, including prefix-rich multi-byte padding
  displayed as DATA16 or CS NOP. Do not count a prefix as a second
  instruction.
- "Halt the processor when privileged or fault when forbidden"
  is HLT. This appears in executable startup/teardown scaffolding
  and is *not* an ordinary unprivileged return.
- "Mark a valid indirect branch target under enabled control-flow
  enforcement" is ENDBR64; feature configuration remains distinct.

Integer values and status:
- "Add the source integer to the destination integer modulo the
  destination width and update the defined arithmetic flags" is ADD.
- "Subtract the source integer from the destination integer modulo
  destination width and update the defined arithmetic flags" is SUB.
- "Compute integer equality/order flags from a subtraction without
  writing back the arithmetic result" is CMP.
- "Compute condition flags from a bitwise AND without storing the
  result" is TEST (and OF/CF are cleared, AF undefined).
- "Store the bitwise exclusive-or of the operands and update the
  defined logic flags" is XOR. XOR of a register with itself is a
  zeroing idiom but still changes flags, unlike MOV.
- "Store the bitwise AND and update the defined logic flags" is AND.
- "Complement every bit of the destination without changing status
  flags" is NOT.
- "Multiply signed integers and store the architecturally selected
  low result, reporting overflow through CF/OF" is IMUL. The one-,
  two-, and three-operand forms are distinct and require the encoded
  form to choose the destination and high-product handling.
- "Shift bits left and fill vacated low bits with zero" is SHL.
- "Shift bits right while preserving the original sign" is SAR.
- "Shift bits right and fill vacated high bits with zero" is SHR.
  Effective count masking, count zero and OF/CF corner cases cannot
  be omitted from a final state transformer.
- "Swap the two operand values" is XCHG, including implicit
  atomicity for memory form.
- "Convert the signed 32-bit integer in EAX to a signed 64-bit
  integer in RAX" is CDQE.

Conditional transfer:
- JE = "branch if the last relevant comparison establishes equality
  (zero flag set)".
- JNE = "branch if the zero flag is clear".
- JA = "branch if unsigned strictly above (carry=0 and zero=0)".
- JAE = "branch if unsigned above or equal (carry=0)".
- JBE = "branch if unsigned below or equal (carry=1 or zero=1)".
- JG = "branch if signed strictly greater (zero=0 and sign=overflow)".
- JGE = "branch if signed greater or equal (sign=overflow)".
- JL = "branch if signed less (sign != overflow)".
- JLE = "branch if signed less or equal (zero=1 or sign != overflow)".
- JP = "branch if parity flag is set"; immediately after a
  scalar floating comparison it detects an *unordered* comparison.
- CMOVS, CMOVNS, CMOVE, CMOVLE, CMOVG = "select a register value
  conditionally without branching." When the condition is false, only
  the destination low-width value is selected again. A 32-bit CMOV
  destination ALWAYS zero-extends into its encompassing 64-bit register,
  including on a false condition; a 64-bit CMOV destination preserves
  all 64 bits on a false condition. A memory source can still be read
  and fault regardless of the condition. RFLAGS are unchanged.
- SETA = "write one byte representing whether unsigned above is true".

Scalar floating values and status:
- MULSD/ADDSD/SUBSD/DIVSD = "multiply/add/subtract/divide low
  double-precision floating lanes according to the floating-point
  environment, preserving the unchanged upper lane as prescribed
  by the exact legacy encoding". They are not exact real arithmetic.
- SQRTSD = "compute the appropriately rounded double-precision
  square root of the low source lane".
- MAXSD = "choose low-lane maximum according to the instruction's
  legacy operand-order rules for NaN and signed-zero ties"; not
  a generic commutative mathematical maximum.
- UCOMISD/COMISD = "compare two low double-precision lanes and write
  zero/carry/parity flags; unordered (NaN) sets all three"; the two
  forms differ in invalid-operation exception signaling for NaNs.
  OF/SF/AF are cleared for a completed comparison.
- CVTSI2SD = "convert signed integer to low double-precision lane
  subject to rounding".

Packed vector-bit transformations:
- PXOR/XORPD = "bitwise exclusive-or the full vector payload".
- ANDPD = "bitwise AND all vector bits".
- ANDNPD = "bitwise AND the bitwise complement of the OLD
  destination bits with source bits".
- ORPD = "bitwise OR all vector bits".
- UNPCKLPD = "interleave the low double-lane of the old destination
  with the low double-lane of the source into the two result lanes".
  These are 128-bit bit-pattern operations, not necessarily
  operations on numeric double values.

What we can learn before examining any Fortran mathematics
----------------------------------------------------------
1. A register movement often prepares arguments to a later call.
2. A conditional branch selects a machine path based on specific
   flags, not a vague "true/false".
3. An SSE data movement can preserve, clear or transform unrelated
   high bits, so it may not equal a scalar-language assignment.
4. A memory address in parentheses/brackets is not automatically an
   accessed matrix element: LEA is explicitly an address calculation.
5. A function call is observable through its calling boundary,
   but a symbol name is not proof of its internal machine effects.
6. A source-level loop may be radically reorganized by optimization.
   Reconstruct algorithm structure *after* inspecting the machine
   paths and the source correspondence, not before.

Choices to keep visible
-----------------------
Option 1 — Translate x86-64 bytes directly to English *first*.
  Advantage: faithful to the archived executable and shows every
  implementation-specific memory and control-flow decision.
  Cost: long, platform-specific and dependent on linker/runtime.
  DECISION: use this now.
Option 2 — Translate Fortran statements first and decorate with
  imagined assembly. Advantage: shorter numerical overview.
  Cost: disguises the compiler's actual decisions.
  DECISION: retain Fortran as a later cross-check, not an oracle.
Option 3 — Introduce a typed, architecture-neutral micro-operation
  representation with explicit flags, memory and effects.
  Advantage: potential shared frontend/backend and equivalence tests.
  Cost: requires chosen semantics for undefined cases, SSE status,
  ABI and executable storage; cannot assume portable meaning.
  DECISION: exploration later, not silently implemented now.
Option 4 — Recompile Fortran for ARM with the Android NDK and inspect
  new real disassembly. Advantage: a valid ARM machine specimen.
  Cost: not the same x86 instructions and not byte-equivalent;
  numerical behavior can vary across BLAS/FP environments.
  DECISION: a separate optional experiment only after current work.
Option 5 — Emulate the exact x86-64 instruction stream under an
  explicit target model. Advantage: concrete state-based oracle.
  Cost: needs all memory mappings, library calls and exception
  behavior; cross-check emulated and native boundary.
  DECISION: a future validation mechanism, not claimed.
Option 6 — Ask a language model to write per-instruction prose and
  mechanically verify only address/byte alignment. Advantage:
  rapid inspection-first concordance. Cost: matching bytes
  does NOT mean semantic sentences are correct.
  DECISION: permissible as an *explicitly unverified* first pass.

This list deliberately exposes rejected and deferred approaches
instead of rewriting the history as if only one possible language
design existed.

Unresolved boundaries for complete specification
------------------------------------------------
- Full Intel opcode/REX/prefix decoding and operand-size legality;
  machine bytes must independently corroborate the mnemonic.
- Precise effects on ALL status bits, including undefined outcomes
  and zero-shift/overflow conditions.
- Floating-point control/exception state, NaNs and signed zero.
- Vector memory alignment, page permissions and possible faults.
- C and Fortran calling contract, by-reference arguments, return
  values, stack alignment, unwind/debug metadata and special cases.
- Static relocations, GOT/PLT behavior and dynamic loader results.
- Execution of BLAS and libgfortran code; whether the complete
  required implementation is even captured.
- A specified startup machine state and observed full dynamic
  path for the smoke fixture, not just an archived output.
- An ARM instruction correspondence with explicit source-target
  correctness conditions, not a rename of opcodes.

No text in this document overrides the preserved raw listing.
